omit-lockfile-registry-resolved=true

# `prefer-offline=true` used to live here. It spared an install from re-fetching a
# packument per dependency: the lockfile carries no `resolved` URLs (above), and
# this network's proxy strips ETag/Last-Modified and answers `Cache-Control:
# no-cache`, so npm has no cheap way to revalidate one.
#
# It cost far more than it saved. `prefer-offline` does not merely prefer the
# cache, it bypasses the staleness check altogether, which makes a cached packument
# the last word forever. Every version published after that cache entry was written
# is then invisible, so a lockfile that moves forward can never be installed: npm
# reports ETARGET / `404 No match found` for a version the registry is serving
# perfectly well, which reads as a deleted package and sends you looking in the
# wrong place. Measured here: the cache insisted `hono` stopped at 4.13.1 and that
# `@hono/node-server@2.1.1` did not exist, while the proxy was serving 4.13.3 and
# 2.1.1 the whole time.
#
# Revalidating costs about a minute on a full install. Leave it removed.
